As of **16 August 2026**, Sanskrit MT evaluation is still fragmented. There is **not yet a GLUE/MMLU-style canonical “Sanskrit Translation Benchmark”** covering the linguistic history of Sanskrit. But there are now enough pieces to build one. The major change is **Mitrasaṃgraha (2026)**: it finally gives Sanskrit→English a genuinely diachronic benchmark spanning Vedic, Epic, Classical and Medieval material. ([arXiv][1])

The landscape looks like this:

| Resource                      | Pair                   | Main coverage                 | What it actually tests          |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| **Mitrasaṃgraha**             | Sanskrit→English       | Vedic → Medieval, many genres | Best broad historical benchmark |
| **Itihāsa**                   | Sanskrit→English       | Mahābhārata + Rāmāyaṇa        | Epic verse                      |
| **Sāmayik**                   | English↔Sanskrit       | contemporary prose            | Modern Sanskrit                 |
| **Samasāmayik**               | Hindi↔Sanskrit         | contemporary prose            | Modern Sanskrit                 |
| **Santham**                   | Sanskrit↔Tamil         | prose/verse, anvaya           | Segmentation + reordering       |
| **Rao–Gopinath probes**       | Sa↔En                  | linguistic phenomena          | Diagnostic grammar tests        |
| **Chandomitra**               | English→Sanskrit       | anuṣṭubh                      | Semantics + metrical generation |
| **SansTib**                   | Sanskrit↔Tibetan       | Buddhist                      | Buddhist parallelism/alignment  |
| **MITRA**                     | Sa/Pāli/Tib/Ch→English | Buddhist + classical          | Classical multilingual MT       |
| **FLORES/IN22/IndicGenBench** | multilingual           | contemporary translated text  | Generic Sanskrit ability        |
| **Bhagavad Gītā studies**     | Sanskrit→English       | Gītā                          | narrow LLM/Google comparisons   |

## 1. Mitrasaṃgraha — currently the most important Sanskrit benchmark

**Nehrdich et al., 2026 — “Mitrasaṃgraha: A Comprehensive Classical Sanskrit Machine Translation Dataset”**

Paper:
`https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07314`

HTML:
`https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07314`

PDF:
`https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.07314`

Dataset/code:
`https://github.com/dharmamitra/mitrasamgraha-dataset`

This is the thing to build on.

It contains **391,548 Sanskrit-English bitext pairs**, plus **5,587 validation** and **5,552 manually post-corrected test pairs**, with metadata for work, period and genre. It deliberately spans more than three millennia rather than pretending Sanskrit is one homogeneous language. ([arXiv][1])

Its contents are unusually valuable. Examples include:

* **Vedic:** Ṛgveda 11,645 pairs, Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda 7,501, Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa 6,193, Sāmaveda 1,343, Śukla Yajurveda 1,776.
* **Epic:** Mahābhārata 90,789 plus associated Epic-era works.
* **Kāvya:** Raghuvaṃśa, Kumārasambhava, Meghadūta, Kāvyādarśa.
* **Mīmāṃsā/Nyāya:** Tantravārttika 6,171 and Nyāyamañjarī 7,763.
* **Buddhist philosophy:** Tattvasaṃgraha, Tattvasaṃgrahapañjikā, Madhyāntavibhāga materials, Triṃśikā, Viṃśatikā, Pañcaskandhaprakaraṇa.
* **Ayurveda:** Suśrutasaṃhitā 10,233.
* **Purāṇic:** Śiva, Skanda, Viṣṇu and other Purāṇas.
* **Śaiva Tantra:** **Tantrāloka: 4,550 pairs**.
* **Later scholastic:** Sarvadarśanasaṃgraha and other medieval works. ([arXiv][1])

That last point matters: **Tantrāloka is already inside the strongest published Sanskrit MT benchmark.** But there is currently no separate benchmark specifically designed around Trika terminology, Abhinavagupta's argumentative prose, commentary boundaries, technical polysemy, etc. Mitrasaṃgraha merely treats it as one work/domain among many. ([arXiv][1])

### Its most important finding is actually about evaluation

They took **seven high-quality human translations of 100 Bhagavad-gītā verses** and compared them pairwise.

Average human-vs-human scores were only:

* BLEU: **25.2**
* chrF: **44.0**

In other words, even good humans look surprisingly “wrong” to lexical-overlap metrics because valid Sanskrit translations diverge dramatically in wording. ([arXiv][1])

They then compared automatic metrics against expert judgments:

**BLEURT and GEMBA/GEMBA*** correlated substantially better than BLEU/chrF. GEMBA* is particularly interesting because it evaluates the Sanskrit source against the candidate **without requiring a reference translation**. ([arXiv][1])

That is probably the single most important empirical result for any Sanskrit translation-evaluation project right now.

