# 2. Itihāsa — the classic Epic benchmark

**Aralikatte et al., 2021 — “Itihasa: A large-scale corpus for Sanskrit to English translation”**

ACL:

`https://aclanthology.org/2021.wat-1.22/`

PDF:

`https://aclanthology.org/2021.wat-1.22.pdf`

GitHub:

`https://github.com/rahular/itihasa`

It contains **93,000 Sanskrit śloka–English translation pairs** from the **Rāmāyaṇa and Mahābhārata** and benchmarks standard MT architectures on them. ([ACL Anthology][2])

This was the major Sanskrit→English resource before Mitrasaṃgraha.

It's still useful because it gives you a reasonably coherent test of:

**Epic Sanskrit + śloka syntax + verse compression + freer word order.**

But don't call performance on Itihāsa “Sanskrit translation performance.” It tells you very little about Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa, Navya-Nyāya, Buddhist scholastic Sanskrit or Abhinavagupta.

