# 8. SansTib — Buddhist Sanskrit deserves its own category

**Sebastian Nehrdich, 2022**

Paper:

`https://aclanthology.org/2022.lrec-1.724/`

PDF:

`https://aclanthology.org/2022.lrec-1.724.pdf`

GitHub:

`https://github.com/sebastian-nehrdich/sanstib`

It contains about **317,289 Sanskrit–Classical Tibetan sentence pairs** from Buddhist texts. It additionally contains three manually aligned gold datasets totalling **6,916 pairs**, enabling proper alignment/translation evaluation rather than relying entirely on automatically mined pairs. ([ACL Anthology][8])

This matters enormously because **Buddhist Sanskrit should not simply be another random slice of “Classical Sanskrit.”**

If you want model capability on Buddhist literature, evaluate that independently.

