# 10. Generic multilingual Sanskrit benchmarks

These are useful for evaluating language coverage, but **do not confuse them with historical Sanskrit benchmarks**.

### IndicGenBench / FLORES-IN

Paper:

`https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16816`

GitHub:

`https://github.com/google-research-datasets/indic-gen-bench`

IndicGenBench evaluates generation over **29 Indic languages**, including translation through FLORES-IN, alongside summarization and QA. ([arXiv][10])

### IndicTrans2 / IN22

Paper:

`https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16307`

GitHub:

`https://github.com/AI4Bharat/IndicTrans2`

IN22 was constructed as an n-way benchmark across India's 22 scheduled languages, with Wiki/Web/general and conversational subsets. ([arXiv][11])

These answer:

> “Can this multilingual model translate Sanskrit-like modern sentences?”

They emphatically do **not** answer:

> “Can this model translate a Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa ritual passage?”

or

> “Does it understand Utpaladeva's technical use of vimarśa?”

