# Now the really important part: how should Sanskrit MT actually be evaluated?

The wider MT-evaluation literature has advanced well beyond BLEU.

## The foundational metric stack

| Method      | Idea                            | Sanskrit use                 |
| ----------- | ------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| BLEU        | word n-gram overlap             | legacy compatibility only    |
| chrF/chrF++ | character n-grams               | useful secondary metric      |
| BERTScore   | contextual embedding similarity | semantic similarity          |
| BLEURT      | learned human-judgment metric   | **strong Sanskrit evidence** |
| COMET       | source + hypothesis + reference | strong modern MT metric      |
| xCOMET      | score + localized errors        | excellent audit layer        |
| MetricX     | neural translation evaluator    | strong current baseline      |
| GEMBA       | LLM-as-judge                    | **strong Sanskrit evidence** |
| MQM         | expert error annotation         | human gold standard          |

### BLEU

Papineni et al.:

`https://aclanthology.org/P02-1040/`

### chrF

`https://aclanthology.org/W15-3049/`

### chrF++

`https://aclanthology.org/W17-4770/`

### SacreBLEU

`https://aclanthology.org/W18-6319/`

If you publish BLEU/chrF at all, use SacreBLEU so preprocessing and tokenization are reproducible.

### BERTScore

`https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09675`

### BLEURT

`https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.04696`

This matters particularly because Mitrasaṃgraha's Sanskrit-specific study found BLEURT much closer to expert judgments than BLEU/chrF. ([arXiv][1])

### COMET

`https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.09025`

COMET learns prediction of human MT judgments using source, translation and reference rather than simple surface matching. ([arXiv][16])

### xCOMET

`https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10482`

Particularly valuable because it aims to produce **fine-grained error spans**, not merely one mysterious quality score.

### GEMBA

`https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.14520`

And GEMBA-MQM:

`https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13988`

This is directly relevant because Mitrasaṃgraha experimentally validates GEMBA/GEMBA* on Sanskrit expert judgments. ([arXiv][1])

